PART 3 — THE VERIFIABLE AGENCY THEOREM

Theorem. Under assumptions A1–A7, the behavior set B of an agentic ST-T1024 is:

1. Finite — |B| < ∞.
2. Constructible — B can be enumerated by a terminating procedure.
3. Envelope-compliant — every action sequence in B respects its declared envelope.

---

PART 4 — PROOF

Lemma 1 (Bounded Trace Length).

Every trace τ has length |τ| ≤ L for some finite L.

Proof. By A4, every trace terminates within T_max cycles. By A1 and the FSM cycle definition (A1 §2.4), each FSM step consumes at least c_min cycles (c_min ≥ 1). Therefore |τ| ≤ ⌊T_max / c_min⌋ = L. □

Note: L is finite but may be large. Finiteness is what matters; size is handled by symbolic methods, not enumeration.

Lemma 2 (Bounded Action Branching).

At every state s ∈ S, the number of actions available to α is bounded by |A|.

Proof. By Definition 6, α(s) ∈ A ∪ {⊥, REFUSE}. The available action set is a subset of A. By A2, |A| is finite. Therefore branching factor at any state is ≤ |A| + 2. □

Lemma 3 (Bounded Plan Depth).

Any execution of a plan p ∈ P produces at most K × D sub-tasks, where K is the max plan length and D is the max invocation depth.

Proof. By A3, each plan has at most K steps. By A6, tile invocation depth is bounded by D. By A7, composition introduces no unbounded recursion. Therefore total sub-tasks per plan ≤ K × D. □

Lemma 4 (Finite Action Alphabet).

The set of action symbols occurring in any trace is a subset of A, which is finite.

Proof. Immediate from Definition 6 and A2. □

Proof of (1) — Finiteness.

By Lemma 1, every trace τ has |τ| ≤ L. By Lemma 3, actions selected within a plan are bounded by K × D. Together, the maximum number of actions in any trace is bounded by N_max = L · K · D.

Every action sequence in B is therefore a finite sequence over a finite alphabet (Lemma 4) of length at most N_max. The set of such sequences is:

B ⊆ ⋃_{l=0}^{N_max} A^l

The right-hand side is a finite union of finite Cartesian powers of a finite set. Therefore B is finite. ∎

Proof of (2) — Constructibility.

By finiteness (1), B is a finite set. By Definitions 1–8, the transition function δ, the selection function α, and the VERIFY gate V are all deterministic and decidable in bounded time (A5). Therefore a breadth-first traversal from the initial state s₀ — enumerating reachable (state, action) pairs and pruning when T_max is exceeded — terminates and outputs exactly B. ∎

Note: Constructibility in principle ≠ tractability in practice. The theorem claims finiteness. Enumeration may be astronomically large. In practice, model-checking uses symbolic methods (BDDs, SMT, TLA+ model-checking) that operate on the structure of B without enumerating it. Finiteness is what licenses these methods.

Proof of (3) — Envelope Compliance.

Let τ = ⟨(s₀, a₀), …, (sₗ, aₗ)⟩ be any valid trace. For each step i, aᵢ = α(sᵢ). By Definition 7, the FSM transitions to COMMIT(aᵢ) only if V(sᵢ, aᵢ) = PASS. By Definition 8, V(sᵢ, aᵢ) = PASS ⇒ sᵢ ∈ E(aᵢ). Therefore every committed action aᵢ is committed from a state within its envelope. Since τ is arbitrary, this holds for every trace, and therefore for every action sequence in B. ∎

Theorem established. ∎

