PART 5 — COROLLARIES

Corollary 1 (No Emergence). Any behavior not in B is impossible.

Proof. By Definition 10, B is the set of all action sequences producible by the agent. Nothing outside B can occur. ∎

Corollary 2 (Exhaustive Verifiability). Any safety property P over B is decidable by finite model-checking.

Proof. B is finite (Theorem 1) and constructible (Theorem 2). A finite set admits decision procedures for any decidable property over its elements. ∎

Corollary 3 (Refusal Completeness). If no action in A satisfies the goal within the confidence threshold, the FSM transitions to 0x-AMBIG-GOAL or 0x-UNSAFE-ACT and halts.

Proof. By Definition 6, α(s) ∈ A ∪ {⊥, REFUSE}. When no action is enabled, α(s) = REFUSE. By A1, the FSM halts on REFUSE. The refusal is explicit and logged (A7). ∎

Corollary 4 (Auditability). Every trace is finite and every step is recorded in the append-only log with an SK-1024 signature.

Proof. Finiteness from Theorem 1. Append-only property from A7. Signature from the SK-1024 protocol (base standard §5). ∎

Corollary 5 (Determinism of Behavior). Given a fixed input sequence σ ∈ Σ*, the agent produces exactly one trace.

Proof. By Definition 5, δ is a function, not a relation. By Definition 6, α is a function. Composition of functions is a function. Therefore the trace is uniquely determined by σ. ∎

