PART 8 — THE THREE FIXES, REVISITED

We now return to the frame from Part 0 and state precisely what has been fixed.

Fix 1 — Architecture

Status: Closed by A0–A9.

Two independent engineers who follow A0–A9 produce structurally identical agentic ST-T1024 systems. Same FSM states, same VERIFY gate, same action schema, same plan representation, same state model, same failure codes. The shape is closed.

Fix 2 — Behavior

Status: Deliberately open.

The standard fixes the grammar of behavior. It does not fix the content. Automotive agents and grid agents share zero actions and zero plans. They share the same schema. This is correct: a standard that fixed content would be a product, not a standard.

The freedom of Fix 2 is enabled by the closure of Fix 1. Because the shape is closed, the content can safely vary.

Fix 3 — Safety

Status: Closed — conditional on machine-checking.

The Verifiable Agency Theorem states and proves that the behavior set B is finite, constructible, and envelope-compliant. This converts safety from a claim into a theorem.

But: the proof is a paper proof. Its assumptions are architectural. Its machine verification is pending. The theorem is closed in principle; it becomes load-bearing when the TLA+ specification is model-checked and — for full generality — discharged in a proof assistant.

That is the honest state.

